  :adr/title "cloud-murakumo-site: the pages had no correct home, so each repo kept a copy"
  :adr/status "accepted"
  :adr/date "2026-09-10"
  :adr/related ["adr-2609102400-a-guard-aimed-at-a-shadowed-file"]
  :adr/body
  "Owner instruction 2026-09-10: create `cloud-murakumo-site`. It resolves the
ownership question ADR-2609102400 left open, and the measurement explains why
the duplication happened at all rather than merely that it did.

## The layering nobody had drawn

`cloud_murakumo/site/*.cljc` existed in BOTH `cloud-murakumo` and
`cloud-murakumo-app`, both on the `:site` classpath, one shadowing the other.
The question was which repo should own them. Neither, as it turns out:

    cloud-murakumo        deploy, the site Worker, the generator
            |
    cloud-murakumo-site   the pages                     <- new
            |
    cloud-murakumo-app    storefront, spec, fleet-state, gen, insight,
                          fleet-metrics

Every namespace the pages reach outside `site/` -- all six of them -- lives in
`cloud-murakumo-app`, and NONE in `cloud-murakumo`. So the pages sit above the
domain and below the deploy, and there was no repo at that layer. Each of the
two adjacent repos kept a copy because each needed them and neither could own
them. The duplication was a missing layer, not carelessness.

## What moved and what did not

Moved (16 `.cljc`): the pages, including `fleet.cljc` and `giemon.cljc`, which
existed only in cloud-murakumo-app and therefore did render.

Not moved:

- `generate.clj` -- the generator, not a page. It stays with what runs it.
- `legal.clj`, `landing_locales.clj`, `trust.clj` -- `.clj`, and ADR-2608201300
  forbids placing new production `.clj`. They stay where they already are.

For the six divergent files the canonical copy is `cloud-murakumo`'s, because
it is the one that rendered and it was current to 09-09 across 68 commits in
thirty days. The sixteen commits `cloud-murakumo-app` made in the same window
went into files that never rendered; that work is not deleted -- it is in that
repo's history and can be ported deliberately, which is the opposite of how it
arrived.

## The proof, and what it caught

All 61 generated pages were hashed before the move and after it, and compared
byte for byte. They are identical.

It took three attempts, and each failure was worth more than the pass:

1. Removing only `cloud-murakumo`'s copy broke the build with
   `No such var: market/locales`. With one copy gone, the OTHER stale copy won
   the classpath -- proving that removing one side does not fix shadowing.
2. After removing both, the same error persisted from a stale `.cpcache`, the
   trap CLAUDE.md already records.
3. The first byte comparison then failed on `docs.html` and `free.html`,
   because the new repo had been seeded from the shared `cloud-murakumo`
   working tree, which was **six commits behind origin/main** and carried a
   docs page missing an hour-old change. Re-seeded from `origin/main` of both
   repos.

A comparison that had only ever passed would have told me none of that. The
third failure is the one worth keeping: seeding a new repo from a working tree
is how a fresh repo is born stale.

## Registered

`repos.edn :extra-projects` + `gen-west-manifest.cljs --entry
cloud-murakumo-site`, pin verified reachable from main; `cloud-murakumo` and
`cloud-murakumo-app` pins advanced in the same commit, both fast-forward.

## What the move then broke, and how it was found

Both halves of the guard work landed red, and a closing audit found them rather
than the commits that caused them. Both are the same mistake:

- `llms_public_api_test.mjs` kept reading `../src/cloud_murakumo/site/docs.cljc`
  after the pages moved, so the whole file threw ENOENT. That is the SECOND
  time this one line has been wrong, in opposite directions -- it previously
  pointed at cloud-murakumo-app because a copy had been mistaken for a move,
  and read a file nothing rendered while passing for weeks.
- `dead_hosted_model.test.mjs` pinned `return await legacyWorker.fetch(await
  rewriteDeadHostedModel(` as a literal, and the very NEXT commit changed the
  statement into `const answered = ...` so that `augmentModels` could see the
  response. A correct change that read as breakage, shipped because that commit
  ran the hokusai suite and not this one.

**When a file moves, or an expression a guard names changes, that guard moves
in the same commit and its suite runs before the commit lands.** Running the
suite that covers what you just wrote is not the same as running the suite that
names what you just moved.

Repaired: the site test follows the pages to cloud-murakumo-site and refuses
with a reason if the sibling is absent, rather than throwing a raw ENOENT; the
API guard pins the call shape rather than how its result is bound, and still
fails when the public fall-through is unwired.

## Follow-up

- `test/shadowed-site-sources.json` is now EMPTY and its test guards the way
  back: a page copied into `cloud-murakumo` or `cloud-murakumo-app` would
  rejoin the `:site` classpath and silently stop one copy rendering. It reads
  working trees rather than main, because that is what `:local/root`
  resolves -- a stale sibling really would shadow, and it caught exactly that
  during this closing.
- `insight.cljc` in cloud-murakumo-app says in a docstring that
  `cloud-murakumo.site.onprem-3d/locale-order` is 正本 and that it holds a
  copy. That is a second, smaller duplication of the same kind, unmeasured."}]
